.TH INTERGENOS-BOOTORDER-CHECK 8 "2026-08-04" "InterGenOS v1.0" "InterGenOS System Administration"
.SH NAME
intergenos-bootorder-check \- keep the registered UEFI boot entry first in the boot order
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B intergenos-bootorder-check
.RB [ \-\-intent-file
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-efibootmgr
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-efi-dir
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-label
.IR LABEL ]
.RB [ \-\-dry-run ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B intergenos-bootorder-check
compares the UEFI boot order held in firmware with the boot entry the
installer registered, and restores that entry to the front of the order when
the installation recorded that InterGenOS is meant to be the default boot
target.
.PP
The installer registers the boot entry with
.BR efibootmgr (8),
which places the new entry first. Some firmware then enumerates the
removable-media fallback loader
.RB ( \eEFI\eBOOT\eBOOTX64.EFI )
on the same EFI system partition as a boot option of its own and places that
option ahead of the registered entry. Both options reach the same partition,
so the machine still boots, but it boots through the fallback loader rather
than the registered one \(em and on a machine carrying more than one operating
system, the difference selects a different loader than the one the
installation registered.
.PP
The tool is run at every boot by
.BR intergenos-bootorder-check.service (8).
It may also be run by hand at any time.
.SH USER CONTROL
The tool changes the boot order only when
.I /etc/intergenos/boot-default.conf
contains
.BR default_boot_target=yes ,
which the installer writes from the answer given during installation. When
the answer was
.BR no \(em
another operating system was kept as the default \(em the tool reports the
current order and changes nothing. When the file is absent, the tool reports
and changes nothing.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-intent-file " PATH"
Read the default-boot-target record from
.I PATH
instead of
.IR /etc/intergenos/boot-default.conf .
.TP
.BI \-\-efibootmgr " PATH"
Use the named
.BR efibootmgr (8)
binary.
.TP
.BI \-\-efi-dir " PATH"
Treat
.I PATH
as the directory whose presence marks an EFI system, instead of
.IR /sys/firmware/efi .
.TP
.BI \-\-label " LABEL"
Keep the entry with this label first, instead of the label recorded in the
intent file.
.TP
.B \-\-dry-run
Report what would change and write nothing to firmware variables.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/intergenos/boot-default.conf
Written by the installer.
.B default_boot_target
is
.B yes
or
.BR no ;
.B boot_entry_label
names the boot entry;
.B foreign_os_entries_at_install
records how many other on-disk operating-system entries the firmware held
when the system was installed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The order already had the entry first, the order was repaired, or the state
could not be determined (no EFI firmware, no
.BR efibootmgr ,
no BootOrder variable, no registered entry). A state that cannot be read is
reported as undetermined, never as a pass and never as a failure.
.TP
.B 1
A repair was written and the firmware still does not report the entry first.
The firmware is overriding the boot order; select InterGenOS in the
firmware's own boot-option menu.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR efibootmgr (8),
.BR forge (1)
